# Blocks a bare `$VAR` — assigned from a command substitution — being passed to a
# command that takes a LIST of files.
#
# NOT once-per-session, like its two zsh siblings: a mechanical mistake with a
# silent failure mode, not a pointer to a document.
#
# WHY. bash word-splits an unquoted `$VAR`; zsh does NOT. So the idiom every
# shell tutorial teaches —
#
#     FILES=$(git diff --name-only)
#     eslint --fix $FILES
#
# — hands zsh ONE argument containing newlines instead of N paths. The command
# then runs against a path that cannot exist, and most tools treat "no such file"
# as nothing to do and exit 0. The failure is silent and reads exactly like
# success, which is the same shape as its two siblings: `no matches found` from
# an unquoted glob, and `wc -l` exiting 0 on empty input.
#
# Three incidents, and the third is why this exists rather than another sentence
# in CLAUDE.md:
#   - a 7-path `git rm $PATHS` deleted nothing; the echo after it announced success
#   - the same shape again, same session
#   - 2026-09-01: `npx eslint --fix $FILES` over 18 files fixed NOTHING. The
#     recount was unchanged, which looked like "these warnings are not
#     auto-fixable" — a wrong conclusion, not just a wasted step. Re-run through
#     xargs it fixed all 18 and went five BELOW the baseline.
#
# CLAUDE.md has named this since August and it was still hit a third time by the
# session that had just read it. That is the argument for mechanising it.
#
# SCOPE IS DELIBERATELY NARROW — two conditions, both required:
#   1. the variable was assigned from `$(...)`, so it plausibly holds a list
#   2. it is passed bare to a command known to take many file arguments
# `n=$(wc -l < f); [ $n -gt 0 ]` does NOT fire: `[` is not in the command list.
# `echo $VAR` does not fire. A quoted `"$VAR"` never fires — that is the correct
# form when one argument is meant.
#
# THE FIX IS ALWAYS THE SAME: write the list to a file and pipe it through xargs,
# or quote it if a single argument was intended.
rule_id=unsplit-var
rule_once=0

rule_match() {
    [ "$1" = "Bash" ] || return 1
    local cmd="$2" name rest
    # Commands that take a LIST of paths. A bare list-valued variable here is
    # always a bug in zsh; anywhere else it may well be deliberate.
    local tools='(npx[[:space:]]+)?(eslint|prettier|tsc|jest|rm|cp|mv|chmod|cat|grep|wc|head|tail|git[[:space:]]+(rm|add|checkout|restore))'

    # Every NAME in `NAME=$(` or `NAME="$(`.
    for name in $(printf '%s' "$cmd" | grep -oE '\b[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*=\"?\$\(' | sed -E 's/=.*//'); do
        # Bare `$NAME` (not "$NAME", not ${NAME}) somewhere after one of those
        # tools on the same line.
        # `)` and `[` are excluded from the span, and that is what keeps this
        # rule quiet on correct code. The router joins the whole command into ONE
        # line, replacing newlines with spaces, so a tool on one line and a
        # variable on the next end up adjacent. Without `)` in the class,
        # `n=$(wc -l < f); [ $n -gt 0 ]` matched — `wc` from inside the
        # substitution, `$n` from the test after it — which is correct, common
        # code, and exactly the false positive that gets a rule switched off.
        # Excluding `)` stops the span escaping the `$( ... )` it started in.
        #
        # An earlier attempt excluded a backslash instead, on the theory that the
        # newline arrived escaped. It arrives as a space; the class was inert and
        # the proof still failed.
        if printf '%s' "$cmd" | grep -qE "${tools}[^|;&()[]*[[:space:]]\\\$${name}([[:space:]]|\$)"; then
            return 0
        fi
    done
    return 1
}

rule_message() {
    cat <<'MSG'
Blocked: zsh does NOT word-split an unquoted variable.

  FILES=$(git diff --name-only)
  npx eslint --fix $FILES        <- ONE argument containing newlines, not N paths

The command runs against a path that cannot exist, most tools call that nothing
to do, and you get exit 0. It reads exactly like success.

Use xargs, which splits on newlines and is what the list wanted anyway:

  git diff --name-only > "$SCRATCH/files.txt"
  xargs npx eslint --fix < "$SCRATCH/files.txt"

Or quote it, if you really meant ONE argument:

  cp "$SRC" "$DEST"

This has been in CLAUDE.md since August and was hit a third time on 2026-09-01,
where `eslint --fix $FILES` silently fixed nothing and the unchanged recount was
read as "not auto-fixable". Through xargs it fixed all 18 files.

Writing ABOUT the pattern — a commit message or heredoc? Put the text in a file
with Write and pass the file (`git commit -F`), the same workaround the
unquoted-glob rule asks for.
MSG
}
